question:
Given positive integers $m$ and $n \geq m$, determine the largest number of dominoes $(1 \times 2$ or $2 \times 1$ rectangles) that can be placed on a rectangular board with $m$ rows and $2 n$ columns consisting of cells $(1 \times 1$ squares $)$ so that:



(i) each domino covers exactly two adjacent cells of the board;



(ii) no two dominoes overlap;



(iii) no two form a $2 \times 2$ square; and



(iv) the bottom row of the board is completely covered by $n$ dominoes.

response:
The bottom row can be covered by $n$ dominoes in only two ways, as shown in the figure below.

[asy]
size(2cm);
draw((0,0)--(2,0)--(2,2)--(0,2)--cycle);
draw((0,1)--(2,1));
draw((0.5,0)--(1.5,0));
draw((0,0)--(0,1));
draw((1,0)--(1,1));
draw((2,0)--(2,1));
draw((0.5,1)--(1.5,1));
draw((0.5,0)--(0.5,1));
draw((1.5,0)--(1.5,1));
label("1", (0.5,0));
label("2", (1.5,0));
[/asy]

Let $N_{m,n}$ be the maximum number of dominoes that can be placed on an $m \times 2n$ board.  We shall prove that
\begin{align*}
N_{m,n}&=mn, \text{ if }m\text{ is even;} \\
N_{m,n}&=mn-1, \text{ if }m\text{ is odd.}
\end{align*}

If $m$ is even, then the board can be covered by $mn$ dominoes as shown in the figure below.

[asy]
for (int i = 1; i <= 4; ++i) {
  for (int j = 1; j <= 6; ++j) {
    draw((j-1,i-1)--(j,i-1)--(j,i)--(j-1,i)--cycle);
    draw((j-1,i-0.5)--(j,i-0.5));
  }
}
[/asy]

If $m$ is odd, then the bottom row must be covered by $n$ dominoes of type 1, as shown in the figure below.

[asy]
for (int i = 1; i <= 3; ++i) {
  for (int j = 1; j <= 4; ++j) {
    draw((j-1,i-1)--(j,i-1)--(j,i)--(j-1,i)--cycle);
    draw((j-1,i-0.5)--(j,i-0.5));
  }
}
[/asy]

If we cover the remaining $(m-1) \times 2n$ board with $\frac{(m-1)2n}{2} = (m-1)n$ dominoes, then the entire board is covered with $mn$ dominoes and condition (iii) is violated.  Therefore, the maximum number of dominoes is $mn-1$.
</think>

<answer>\boxed{mn-1}</answer>

