The total number of such triples is the sum over all white cells $C_{1}$ of the number of red cells in the same row as $C_{1}$, each counted as many times as the number of white cells in its column.

Now let $A$ be the number of white cells, $B$ the number of red cells and $C$ the number of white-red pairs. Then the number of triples is $T = C(A + B)$.

Since $A+B=999^{2}$, $T$ is maximized when $C$ is maximized. Now $C$ is the number of white-red pairs, which is the total number of pairs minus the number of white-white pairs minus the number of red-red pairs.

Since the total number of pairs is fixed, $C$ is maximized when the number of white-white pairs and red-red pairs is minimized. Since each row and each column contains 999 cells, the number of pairs in each row or column is $999 \times 998 / 2 = 999 \times 499$. Since there are 999 rows and 999 columns, the total number of pairs is $2 \times 999^{2} \times 499$.

The number of white-white pairs is minimized when the white cells are distributed as evenly as possible, so that each row and each column contains either $A/999$ or $(A+999)/999$ white cells. The number of white-white pairs is then $999 \times A/999 \times (A/999 - 1/2) + 999 \times (A + 999)/999 \times ((A + 999)/999 - 1/2) = A(A - 999)/999$.

Similarly, the number of red-red pairs is $B(B - 999)/999$. Hence the number of white-red pairs is $$C = 2 \times 999^{2} \times 499 - \frac{A(A - 999)}{999} - \frac{B(B - 999)}{999} = \frac{999^{2} \times 998 - A^{2} - B^{2}}{999}.$$

