Let $P$ be the point $(2/7,3/7)$.  Reflecting $P$ about the line $y=x$ gives $Q=(3/7,2/7)$.  Then $PQ$ is a path from $P$ to itself that touches two sides of the square, so we can use this path as a baseline.  We want to add a path that touches a third side.

[asy]
draw((0,0)--(1,0)--(1,1)--(0,1)--(0,0));
draw((2/7,3/7)--(1,3/7)--(1,2/7)--(3/7,2/7));
label("$A$", (0,0), SW);
label("$R$", (1,0), SE);
label("$M$", (1,1), NE);
label("$L$", (0,1), NW);
label("$P$", (2/7,3/7), N);
label("$Q$", (3/7,2/7), S);
label("$S$", (1,3/7), N);
[/asy]

Let $S$ be the point $(1,3/7)$.  Then $PS$ is a path from $P$ to $S$ that touches one side of the square.  We can use this path to add to the $PQ$ path.  Note that the segment $SQ$ is the reflection of $PS$ about the line $y=x$.

[asy]
draw((0,0)--(1,0)--(1,1)--(0,1)--(0,0));
draw((2/7,3/7)--(1,3/7)--(1,2/7)--(3/7,2/7));
label("$A$", (0,0), SW);
label("$R$", (1,0), SE);
label("$M$", (1,1), NE);
label("$L$", (0,1), NW);
label("$P$", (2/7,3/7), N);
label("$Q$", (3/7,2/7), S);
label("$S$", (1,3/7), N);
[/asy]

To get a path from $P$ to itself that touches three sides, we can reflect $S$ about the line $x=1$ to get $T=(2,3/7)$.  Then $ST$ is a path from $S$ to $T$ that touches one side of the square.  We can use this path to add to the $PS$ and $SQ$ paths.

[asy]
draw((0,0)--(1,0)--(1,1)--(0,1)--(0,0));
draw((2/7,3/7)--(1,3/7)--(2,3/7)--(1,2/7)--(3/7,2/7));
label("$A$", (0,0), SW);
label("$R$", (1,0), SE);
label("$M$", (1,1), NE);
label("$L$", (0,1), NW);
label("$P$", (2/7,3/7), N);
label("$Q$", (3/7,2/7), S);
label("$S$", (1,3/7), N);
label("$T$", (2,3/7), N);
[/asy]

Finally, we can reflect $T$ about the line $y=x$ to get $U=(3/7,2)$.  Then $TU$ is a path from $T$ to $U$ that touches one side of the square.  We can use this path to add to the $ST$ and $SQ$ paths.

[asy]
draw((0,0)--(1,0)--(1,1)--(0,1)--(0,0));
draw((2/7,3/7)--(1,3/7)--(2,3/7)--(1,2/7)--(3/7,2/7)--(3/7,2));
label("$A$", (0,0), SW);
label("$R$", (1,0), SE);
label("$M$", (1,1), NE);
label("$L$", (0,1), NW);
label("$P$", (2/7,3/7), N);
label("$Q$", (3/7,2/7), S);
label("$S$", (1,3/7), N);
label("$T$", (2,3/7), N);
label("$U$", (3/7,2), S);
[/asy]

Then $PU$ is a path from $P$ to itself that touches three sides of the square.  The length of $PU$ is $\sqrt{(3/7-2/7)^2+(2-3/7)^2} = \sqrt{128}/7 = \boxed{8\sqrt{2}/7}$.
</think>

<answer>\boxed{8\sqrt{2}/7}</answer>

